// ========================================
// CorpHealth Operations Activity Review
// Complete Investigation Query Library with Narrative
// ========================================
// Device: CH-OPS-WKS02
// Investigation Period: November 23 - November 30, 2025
// Case Classification: Operations Activity Review → Confirmed Intrusion
// Lead Analyst: Jarvis
// Platform: Microsoft Defender for Endpoint / Azure Sentinel
// ========================================

// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT
// ===========================================
// Your organization recently deployed CorpHealth, a lightweight system monitoring
// and maintenance framework using scheduled tasks, background services, and 
// diagnostic scripts across operational workstations.
//
// A dedicated operational account (ops.maintenance) was provisioned with local
// administrator privileges to:
// - Register scheduled maintenance tasks
// - Install/remove system services
// - Write diagnostic data to protected locations
// - Perform automated cleanup and telemetry
//
// This account was designed for automation only - NOT interactive sign-ins.
//
// In mid-November, routine monitoring surfaced unusual activity on a workstation
// in the operations environment. Activity appeared consistent with maintenance
// at first glance, but closer review revealed:
// - Activity outside normal maintenance windows
// - Script execution patterns deviating from baselines
// - Manual process launches instead of automation
// - Behaviors resembling credential compromise
//
// You are the lead analyst reviewing historical telemetry to determine:
// - What system was affected
// - When suspicious activity occurred
// - How the activity progressed
// - Whether this was authorized automation or account misuse
// ===========================================

// ===========================================
// FLAG 0: ESTABLISH INVESTIGATION SCOPE
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// Overnight, the monitoring queue tagged a single workstation with a cluster 
// of correlated events. The alerts were low-severity and categorized under 
// "Operational Maintenance Activity (Unclassified)". However, timestamps 
// looked unusual - the sequence occurred during an off-hours window in 
// mid-November when NO maintenance jobs were scheduled.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Confirm which workstation generated the unusual telemetry and establish
// the timeframe you'll be working within for the rest of the investigation.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Need to find a workstation showing:
// - Small cluster of events during unusual maintenance window
// - Activity between Mid-November to Early December
// - Multiple event types (Process, Network, File, Script operations)
// - CorpHealth naming convention (hint: ch-xxx-xxxNN)
// ===========================================

DeviceEvents
| where TimeGenerated between (datetime(2025-11-10) .. datetime(2025-12-05))
| where hourofday(TimeGenerated) between (18 .. 23) or hourofday(TimeGenerated) between (0 .. 6) // Off-hours
| where DeviceName startswith "ch-" // CorpHealth naming convention
| summarize 
    EventCount = count(),
    EventTypes = make_set(ActionType),
    FirstEvent = min(TimeGenerated),
    LastEvent = max(TimeGenerated),
    UniqueProcesses = dcount(InitiatingProcessFileName)
    by DeviceName
| where EventCount > 10 // Clustered activity
| order by EventCount desc

// FINDING: ch-ops-wks02
// SIGNIFICANCE: Single endpoint with concentrated off-hours activity
// NEXT STEP: Investigate what scripts are running on this specific workstation

// ===========================================
// FLAG 1: IDENTIFY UNIQUE MAINTENANCE FILE
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// You've confirmed that CH-OPS-WKS02 is the workstation of interest and 
// narrowed your timeframe to the mid-November maintenance window.
//
// Before digging into outbound activity, you decide to sanity-check what's
// actually running on this box during "routine" operations.
//
// CorpHealth uses a mix of scheduled scripts and diagnostic utilities across
// multiple endpoints. Most are standard and repeated across many machines.
// But one script on CH-OPS-WKS02 appears UNIQUE to this host, tied to 
// recent maintenance work.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify which "maintenance" file stands out here before treating any
// behavior as normal.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Need to compare filenames across devices and find a script that ONLY 
// shows up on CH-OPS-WKS02. Focus on script-like files (.ps1) in 
// maintenance locations.
// ===========================================

DeviceFileEvents
| where TimeGenerated between (datetime(2025-11-10) .. datetime(2025-12-05))
| where FileName endswith ".ps1" // PowerShell scripts
| where FolderPath has_any ("ProgramData", "Windows", "Temp", "AppData") // Maintenance locations
| summarize 
    DeviceCount = dcount(DeviceName),
    Devices = make_set(DeviceName)
    by FileName
| where DeviceCount == 1 // Unique to one device
| where Devices has "ch-ops-wks02"
| project FileName, DeviceCount, Devices

// FINDING: MaintenanceRunner_Distributed.ps1
// SIGNIFICANCE: Script exists ONLY on compromised workstation - not standard deployment
// RED FLAG: Why does this device have a unique maintenance script?
// NEXT STEP: Examine what this script actually does when executed

// ===========================================
// FLAG 2: OUTBOUND BEACON INDICATOR
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// After identifying the unique maintenance script, you now pivot to determine
// what this script actually DID when executed.
//
// CorpHealth agents often phone home to internal listeners or update servers,
// but the behavior from CH-OPS-WKS02 feels different.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Determine when the maintenance script FIRST initiated outbound communication.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Use DeviceNetworkEvents to filter for network activity generated by the
// suspicious script. The script's command line should appear inside
// InitiatingProcessCommandLine. Need the exact timestamp of first beacon.
// ===========================================

DeviceNetworkEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where InitiatingProcessCommandLine contains "MaintenanceRunner_Distributed.ps1"
| project TimeGenerated, RemoteIP, RemotePort, ActionType, InitiatingProcessCommandLine
| order by TimeGenerated asc
| take 1

// FINDING: 2025-11-23T03:46:08.400686Z
// SIGNIFICANCE: First outbound network activity from the suspicious script
// TIMELINE ANCHOR: This is our earliest confirmed malicious activity timestamp
// NEXT STEP: Identify WHERE the script was trying to beacon to

// ===========================================
// FLAG 3: IDENTIFY THE BEACON DESTINATION
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// You've confirmed the suspicious PowerShell activity on CH-OPS-WKS02, and
// the process responsible appears to be tied to a maintenance-related script.
//
// Now leadership wants to know something more urgent:
// WHERE was the workstation trying to beacon to?
//
// Even if the connection failed, the attempt itself can reveal intent:
// - Exfiltration staging
// - Command-and-control signaling
// - Internal lateral movement
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Extract the actual network destination the host attempted to reach.
// This beacon traffic is your first concrete IOC leading off-host.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Filter DeviceNetworkEvents on CH-OPS-WKS02, look for events where
// InitiatingProcessCommandLine contains the maintenance script.
// Need: IP:Port format
// ===========================================

DeviceNetworkEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where InitiatingProcessCommandLine contains "MaintenanceRunner_Distributed.ps1"
| summarize 
    FirstAttempt = min(TimeGenerated),
    AttemptCount = count()
    by RemoteIP, RemotePort
| extend Destination = strcat(RemoteIP, ":", RemotePort)
| order by FirstAttempt asc
| project Destination, FirstAttempt, AttemptCount

// FINDING: 127.0.0.1:8080
// SIGNIFICANCE: Localhost beaconing - possible local proxy or tunneling setup
// CRITICAL: This suggests attacker established local C2 infrastructure
// NEXT STEP: Confirm when the beacon connection actually succeeded

// ===========================================
// FLAG 4: CONFIRM THE SUCCESSFUL BEACON TIMESTAMP
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// Your investigation now has a clearer shape:
// - The host ran a suspicious maintenance script
// - That script attempted to beacon out
// - You've identified the destination IP and port
//
// But now you need to know something more specific and operationally important:
// When did the beacon FINALLY SUCCEED?
//
// Even if some earlier attempts failed, a successful outbound connection
// indicates the earliest point the actor could have received instructions
// or data back from the host.
//
// This timestamp often becomes the pivot point for reconstructing the
// attack timeline.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Isolate the latest (max) ConnectionSuccess event originating from the
// maintenance script, including the remote IP and port.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Filter for ActionType == "ConnectionSuccess"
// Match same RemoteIP and RemotePort from previous flag
// Sort by timestamp descending and take the newest entry
// ===========================================

DeviceNetworkEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where ActionType == "ConnectionSuccess"
| where InitiatingProcessCommandLine contains "MaintenanceRunner_Distributed.ps1"
| where RemoteIP == "127.0.0.1" and RemotePort == 8080
| summarize LatestSuccess = max(TimeGenerated)
| project LatestSuccess

// FINDING: 2025-11-30T01:03:17.6985973Z
// SIGNIFICANCE: Definitive "handshake moment" - attacker could begin issuing commands
// TIMELINE: 7 days after initial beacon attempt - persistent reconnection attempts
// CONCERN: Shortly after this, unusual filesystem activity begins
// NEXT STEP: Investigate staging directories and file artifacts

// ===========================================
// FLAG 5: UNEXPECTED STAGING ACTIVITY DETECTED
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// With the timestamp confirmed, the picture sharpens. You now know exactly
// when the maintenance script successfully reached out to the external IP.
//
// But something isn't sitting right.
//
// Shortly after the successful outbound connection, the workstation's event
// telemetry begins to show unusual activity in directories that normally
// stay quiet. These aren't typical heartbeat logs or scheduled maintenance
// outputs you expect from CH-OPS-WKS02.
//
// Instead, you see artifacts appearing in diagnostic locations, temporary
// export folders, and operational paths - almost as if the attacker is
// preparing the environment for later stages.
//
// Before attackers exfiltrate or execute heavier actions, they almost always
// prepare a workspace - a location on disk to store intermediate files, drop
// transient payloads, or build export bundles.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Determine exactly what artifacts the attacker staged. This will help you
// understand what they intended to take - or alter.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Look for created files under "CorpHealth" operational folders, especially
// Diagnostics directories. Attackers commonly use legitimate paths for staging.
// ===========================================

DeviceFileEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where TimeGenerated >= datetime(2025-11-23T03:46:08Z) // After beacon
| where ActionType == "FileCreated"
| where FolderPath has "CorpHealth" and FolderPath has "Diagnostics"
| where FileName endswith ".csv"
| order by TimeGenerated asc
| project TimeGenerated, FolderPath, FileName, SHA256
| take 1

// FINDING: C:\ProgramData\Microsoft\Diagnostics\CorpHealth\inventory_6ECFD4DF.csv
// SIGNIFICANCE: Attacker staging data in legitimate diagnostic directory
// HIDING IN PLAIN SIGHT: Using official CorpHealth paths to avoid detection
// FILE TYPE: CSV suggests data collection/enumeration
// NEXT STEP: Verify file integrity with cryptographic hash

// ===========================================
// FLAG 6: CONFIRM THE STAGED FILE'S INTEGRITY
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// Now that you've identified the attacker's staging location, you've recovered
// the suspicious file placed in the system's diagnostic directory.
//
// But finding the file is just the beginning.
//
// Analysts rarely trust a recovered artifact at face value - especially one
// dropped during a suspected intrusion.
//
// In real incident response:
// - Hashes help confirm whether files match known malware
// - They allow comparison against threat intelligence feeds
// - They ensure chain-of-custody integrity during analysis
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Extract the SHA-256 hash of the staged file you identified in the previous flag.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Use DeviceFileEvents to retrieve hash metadata for the file.
// This becomes a permanent IOC for threat intelligence sharing.
// ===========================================

DeviceFileEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where FolderPath == @"C:\ProgramData\Microsoft\Diagnostics\CorpHealth"
| where FileName == "inventory_6ECFD4DF.csv"
| where ActionType == "FileCreated"
| project TimeGenerated, FileName, SHA256, FileSize

// FINDING: 7f6393568e414fc564dad6f49a06a161618b50873404503f82c4447d239f12d8
// SIGNIFICANCE: Cryptographic fingerprint established for IOC tracking
// IOC STATUS: Can be compared against threat intel feeds and shared with SOC
// NEXT STEP: Search for duplicate or related staging artifacts

// ===========================================
// FLAG 7: IDENTIFY THE DUPLICATE STAGED ARTIFACT
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// You've confirmed the SHA-256 hash of the attacker's primary staging file.
// Good. But something feels off.
//
// Threat actors often stage redundant or decoy files for several reasons:
// - To confuse responders
// - To test which folders are monitored
// - To discard intermediary versions
// - To probe what security tools detect or ignore
// - To preserve backups of their in-progress work
//
// When you zoom out and look across all file events on CH-OPS-WKS02,
// you notice patterns in the filenames, sizes, and write times.
//
// One particular detail stands out: It looks like another file exists that is:
// - Very similar in name
// - Roughly the same size
// - Written around the same timeframe
// - BUT has a DIFFERENT SHA-256 hash
// - AND is located in a DIFFERENT directory
//
// This is almost certainly an attacker "working copy" or alternate staging location.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Find the second file. Provide the complete absolute path.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Search for other files containing "inventory" created around same timeframe.
// Different hash = different content = iterative data processing
// ===========================================

DeviceFileEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where TimeGenerated >= datetime(2025-11-23T03:46:08Z)
| where FileName contains "inventory"
| where FileName endswith ".csv"
| where SHA256 != "7f6393568e414fc564dad6f49a06a161618b50873404503f82c4447d239f12d8"
| project TimeGenerated, FolderPath, FileName, SHA256
| order by TimeGenerated asc

// FINDING: C:\Users\ops.maintenance\AppData\Local\Temp\CorpHealth\inventory_tmp_6ECFD4DF.csv
// SIGNIFICANCE: Attacker created WORKING COPY in user's temp directory
// PATTERN: Different hash suggests data transformation/filtering before exfiltration
// IMPLICATION: Attacker preparing curated datasets, not raw dumps
// NEXT STEP: Investigate credential access attempts

// ===========================================
// FLAG 8: SUSPICIOUS REGISTRY ACTIVITY
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// You've uncovered something important. Not only did the attacker stage a
// primary diagnostic export in the CorpHealth directory, but you also
// discovered a second, nearly identical file hidden deeper in the user's
// Temp directory.
//
// That's not accidental.
//
// This pattern usually suggests intermediate processing, where the attacker
// may have been transforming or filtering data before exfiltration.
//
// But there's an even more concerning implication:
// If the attacker is staging diagnostic exports and building curated datasets
// in hidden working directories, it raises the question:
//
// What credentials might they have accessed to support the rest of the attack chain?
//
// As you continue reviewing the timeline, a new cluster of events appears -
// this time involving the creation of a suspicious registry key under HKLM.
// The events don't look like standard system operations or anything produced
// by authorized CorpHealth maintenance scripts.
//
// Instead, they strongly resemble credential harvesting or token inspection attempts.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify which exact registry key was created or touched during credential
// harvesting simulation activity.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Look for registry events shortly before/after staging files were created.
// Filter RegistryKeyCreated or RegistryValueSet actions.
// Focus on keys that shouldn't exist on normal workstations.
// ===========================================

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where TimeGenerated between (datetime(2025-11-23T03:45:00Z) .. datetime(2025-11-23T04:00:00Z))
| where ActionType in ("RegistryKeyCreated", "RegistryValueSet")
| where RegistryKey contains "CorpHealth"
| where RegistryKey startswith "HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE"
| project TimeGenerated, ActionType, RegistryKey, RegistryValueName, RegistryValueData
| order by TimeGenerated asc

// FINDING: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\ControlSet001\Services\EventLog\Application\CorpHealthAgent
// SIGNIFICANCE: Attacker creating fake event log source for CorpHealthAgent
// TECHNIQUE: Attempting to blend malicious activity into legitimate logs
// IMPLICATION: Preparation for credential harvesting or log manipulation
// NEXT STEP: Search for scheduled task persistence mechanisms

// ===========================================
// FLAG 9: SCHEDULED TASK PERSISTENCE
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// You continue examining the activity coming from CH-OPS-WKS02.
//
// Moments after the credential-related registry anomaly, you notice
// additional persistence patterns.
//
// Even though some attempts may have failed due to permission issues,
// at least one scheduled task was successfully created earlier in the
// investigation window.
//
// This task is NOT part of CorpHealth's standard approved tasks and
// represents an unauthorized persistence mechanism.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify which Scheduled Task the attacker first created.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Search DeviceRegistryEvents for:
// - ActionType == "RegistryKeyCreated" or "RegistryValueSet"
// - Path contains: Schedule\TaskCache\Tree\
// - Focus on tasks with "CorpHealth" in name (masquerading as legitimate)
// ===========================================

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where ActionType in ("RegistryKeyCreated", "RegistryValueSet")
| where RegistryKey contains @"Schedule\TaskCache\Tree"
| where RegistryKey contains "CorpHealth"
| project TimeGenerated, ActionType, RegistryKey
| order by TimeGenerated asc
| take 1

// FINDING: HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows NT\CurrentVersion\Schedule\TaskCache\Tree\CorpHealth_A65E64
// SIGNIFICANCE: Unauthorized scheduled task masquerading as legitimate CorpHealth maintenance
// PERSISTENCE: Task will survive reboots and execute attacker payload automatically
// NAMING PATTERN: Random hex suffix (A65E64) suggests automated generation
// NEXT STEP: Identify Run key persistence attempts

// ===========================================
// FLAG 10: REGISTRY-BASED PERSISTENCE
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// As your investigation advances, you pivot toward examining whether the
// attacker attempted any form of autostart persistence.
//
// Your earlier findings suggested the script may have tampered with startup
// mechanisms - but now you have confirmation.
//
// You observe:
// - A new Run key value created
// - A value written pointing to execution of a PowerShell script
// - And the value DELETED shortly after
//
// This pattern is intentional - it resembles EPHEMERAL PERSISTENCE, meant to
// survive a single reboot or login, trigger once, and then erase its tracks.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify which value name was added to the Run key.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Filter DeviceRegistryEvents for:
// - RegistryKeyCreated, RegistryValueSet, RegistryValueDeleted
// - Path contains: CurrentVersion\Run
// - Look for rapid create→delete pattern
// ===========================================

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where RegistryKey contains @"CurrentVersion\Run"
| where ActionType in ("RegistryValueSet", "RegistryValueDeleted")
| where RegistryValueName contains "Maintenance"
| project TimeGenerated, ActionType, RegistryKey, RegistryValueName, RegistryValueData
| order by TimeGenerated asc

// FINDING: MaintenanceRunner
// SIGNIFICANCE: Short-lived Run key persistence - added and quickly removed
// PATTERN: Flip-flop behavior suggests attacker TESTING what sticks
// STEALTH: Quick deletion avoids long-term detection
// NEXT STEP: Identify privilege escalation attempts

// ===========================================
// FLAG 11: PRIVILEGE ESCALATION EVENT TIMESTAMP
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// You document the short-lived Run-key persistence, noting how quickly it
// was added and removed. Normally, this kind of flip-flop suggests
// experimentation - an attacker testing what sticks, rather than deploying
// a long-term mechanism.
//
// Before you can pivot to your next hypothesis, another artifact catches
// your eye: a suspicious Application event generated by powershell.exe,
// referencing a ConfigAdjust attempt.
//
// This isn't normal operator behavior. Privilege adjustments are commonly
// probed by attackers who want higher-level access before continuing their
// intrusion.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Locate the exact Timestamp (UTC) of the FIRST ConfigAdjust privilege-
// escalation event.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// This comes from the Application log - not process creation, registry,
// or network events. Look for event payload in AdditionalFields describing
// configuration adjustment.
// ===========================================

DeviceEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where ActionType == "ApplicationLogEvent"
| where AdditionalFields contains "ConfigAdjust"
| project TimeGenerated, ActionType, AdditionalFields
| order by TimeGenerated asc
| take 1

// FINDING: 2025-11-23T03:47:21.8529749Z
// SIGNIFICANCE: Privilege escalation attempt detected
// TIMELINE: ~1 minute after initial beacon (rapid progression)
// TECHNIQUE: ConfigAdjust simulation suggests testing privilege boundaries
// NEXT STEP: Investigate attempts to weaken host defenses

// ===========================================
// FLAG 12: IDENTIFY THE AV EXCLUSION ATTEMPT
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// With persistence mechanisms identified and the timeline growing clearer,
// you shift your attention toward another common phase in intrusions:
// weakening host defenses.
//
// Attackers often attempt to disable or bypass security controls immediately
// before staging files or running higher-risk payloads.
//
// As the hunt progresses, you uncover telemetry suggesting a modification
// attempt targeting endpoint protection settings.
//
// While the change did NOT SUCCEED - likely blocked by permissions - the
// intent is unmistakable.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify exactly what folder path the adversary tried to exclude from
// antivirus scanning.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Look for ExclusionPath in AdditionalFields or registry events related
// to Windows Defender exclusions. Even failed attempts show intent.
// ===========================================

DeviceEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where ActionType contains "AntivirusDetection" or AdditionalFields contains "ExclusionPath"
| extend ExclusionPath = tostring(parse_json(AdditionalFields).ExclusionPath)
| where isnotempty(ExclusionPath)
| project TimeGenerated, ExclusionPath, AdditionalFields
| order by TimeGenerated asc

// Alternative if above doesn't work:
DeviceRegistryEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where RegistryKey contains "Windows Defender" and RegistryKey contains "Exclusions"
| where RegistryValueData contains "staging"
| project TimeGenerated, RegistryKey, RegistryValueName, RegistryValueData

// FINDING: C:\ProgramData\Corp\Ops\staging
// SIGNIFICANCE: Attacker attempted to exclude staging directory from AV scanning
// FAILED: Permission denied, but intent is clear
// IMPLICATION: Attacker knew they would be staging malicious files here
// NEXT STEP: Investigate encoded PowerShell execution

// ===========================================
// FLAG 13: POWERSHELL ENCODED COMMAND EXECUTION
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// You continue unraveling the attacker's chain of activity. After staging
// data, many threat actors pivot into script-based execution - often with
// encoded payloads to evade casual inspection.
//
// Your telemetry reveals that a PowerShell process executed with an
// EncodedCommand, writing out a diagnostic artifact into the CorpHealth folder.
//
// It's time to identify exactly which encoded payload was executed.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Determine what decoded PowerShell command was executed FIRST.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Filter for -EncodedCommand in ProcessCommandLine
// Filter for AccountName = ops.maintenance (avoid system processes)
// Extract and decode Base64 string using KQL base64_decode_tostring()
// PowerShell uses Unicode encoding, so decode accordingly
// ===========================================

DeviceProcessEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where ProcessCommandLine contains "-EncodedCommand"
| where AccountName == "ops.maintenance" // Filter out system processes
| extend EncodedCommand = extract(@"-EncodedCommand\s+([A-Za-z0-9+/=]+)", 1, ProcessCommandLine)
| extend DecodedCommand = base64_decode_tostring(EncodedCommand)
| project TimeGenerated, AccountName, EncodedCommand, DecodedCommand, ProcessCommandLine
| order by TimeGenerated asc
| take 1

// FINDING: Write-Output 'token-6D5E4EE08227'
// SIGNIFICANCE: Attacker executing obfuscated PowerShell to output token
// TECHNIQUE: Base64 encoding to evade signature-based detection
// TOKEN PATTERN: Hexadecimal identifier suggests credential or session token
// NEXT STEP: Investigate token privilege modifications

// ===========================================
// FLAG 14: PRIVILEGE TOKEN MODIFICATION
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// You've traced the suspicious registry activity and the scheduled
// persistence artifacts back to the same PowerShell execution chain.
//
// But the attacker didn't stop there.
//
// As you review deeper system events, something stands out:
// Windows recorded a ProcessPrimaryTokenModified event, a behavior
// consistent with attackers attempting to escalate privileges or adjust
// token integrity to blend in with SYSTEM-level processes.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Pinpoint which process actually performed that token modification.
// Identify the InitiatingProcessId.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Filter DeviceEvents where AdditionalFields contains:
// - "tokenChangeDescription"
// - "Privileges were added"
// Recall the MaintenanceRunner script from Flag 1
// ===========================================

DeviceEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where AdditionalFields contains "tokenChangeDescription" or AdditionalFields contains "Privileges were added"
| extend TokenDetails = parse_json(AdditionalFields)
| project 
    TimeGenerated,
    InitiatingProcessId,
    InitiatingProcessFileName,
    InitiatingProcessCommandLine,
    TokenDetails
| order by TimeGenerated asc

// FINDING: 4888
// SIGNIFICANCE: Process ID 4888 modified its own token privileges
// TECHNIQUE: Classic privilege escalation behavior
// PROCESS: Likely MaintenanceRunner_Distributed.ps1 execution
// NEXT STEP: Identify whose token was actually modified

// ===========================================
// FLAG 15: WHOSE TOKEN WAS MODIFIED?
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// You've confirmed that a process on CH-OPS-WKS02 modified its own token
// privileges - a classic PrivEsc behavior.
//
// But you still don't know whose token was affected.
//
// Digging into the raw event details, you notice that the token modification
// record doesn't just describe what changed; it also tells you which security
// principal (SID) the token belongs to.
//
// That's crucial context:
// - Was this a low-priv user?
// - A domain user?
// - Or a built-in local admin?
//
// If an attacker is adjusting privileges on the local Administrator token,
// that significantly raises the risk profile of any follow-on activity.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Extract the security identifier (SID) the modified token belonged to.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Parse AdditionalFields JSON to find:
// - OriginalTokenUserSid (matches CurrentTokenUserSid in this case)
// This identifies which user account's privileges were elevated
// ===========================================

DeviceEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where AdditionalFields contains "tokenChangeDescription"
| extend TokenInfo = parse_json(AdditionalFields)
| extend 
    OriginalTokenUserSid = tostring(TokenInfo.OriginalTokenUserSid),
    CurrentTokenUserSid = tostring(TokenInfo.CurrentTokenUserSid)
| project 
    TimeGenerated,
    InitiatingProcessId,
    OriginalTokenUserSid,
    CurrentTokenUserSid
| order by TimeGenerated asc
| take 1

// FINDING: S-1-5-21-1605642021-30596605-784192815-1000
// SIGNIFICANCE: SID belongs to local user account (RID 1000)
// IMPLICATION: ops.maintenance account token was modified
// RISK LEVEL: Elevated from standard user to higher privileges
// NEXT STEP: Investigate tool transfer after privilege escalation

// ===========================================
// PHASE TRANSITION: INGRESS TOOL TRANSFER
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// After the token-modification event on CH-OPS-WKS02, the activity on the
// host shifted away from privilege manipulation and toward actions consistent
// with staging follow-on tooling.
//
// Within minutes of the escalation, the workstation initiated outbound
// traffic to an unfamiliar, dynamically generated external domain - an
// unusual pattern for this device and user.
//
// The request originated from curl.exe, which is rarely used interactively
// in this environment.
//
// Shortly afterward, Defender recorded the appearance and execution of a
// new, unsigned binary placed directly in the user's profile directory.
//
// The process opened a local socket and attempted repeated outbound
// connections to an external endpoint hosted on a tunneling service.
//
// These actions suggest the attacker moved quickly from local privilege
// abuse to establishing a remote foothold.
// ===========================================

// ===========================================
// FLAG 16: INGRESS TOOL TRANSFER FROM EXTERNAL DYNAMIC TUNNEL
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// After the privilege escalation, Defender recorded a new executable being
// written to disk on CH-OPS-WKS02.
//
// The timing and location of this file suggest it was delivered as staging
// material for follow-on activity.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify the exact filename the attacker introduced.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Filter DeviceFileEvents by recent .exe writes
// Look for file written into user profile path
// Check which files were created immediately after curl.exe activity
// ===========================================

DeviceFileEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where ActionType == "FileCreated"
| where FileName endswith ".exe"
| where FolderPath has "Users"
| where TimeGenerated >= datetime(2025-11-23T03:47:21Z) // After token modification
| project TimeGenerated, FolderPath, FileName, SHA256, FileSize
| order by TimeGenerated asc
| take 1

// FINDING: revshell.exe
// SIGNIFICANCE: Reverse shell executable downloaded to user profile
// NAMING: Blatant naming suggests confidence in evading detection
// DELIVERY: Downloaded via external tunnel (upcoming flag)
// NEXT STEP: Identify the download source URL

// ===========================================
// FLAG 17: EXTERNAL DOWNLOAD SOURCE
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// Before the suspicious file appeared on the workstation, the host reached
// out to an external dynamic-tunnel domain using curl.exe.
//
// This outbound request fetched the executable later used in post-escalation
// activity.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify the exact remote URL involved in this transfer.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Search for outbound HTTPS activity initiated by curl.exe
// The domain name is long and hyphenated
// It belongs to a tunneling platform (ngrok) often used for temporary
// exposure of local services
// ===========================================

DeviceNetworkEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where InitiatingProcessFileName == "curl.exe"
| where RemoteUrl contains "ngrok"
| where RemoteUrl endswith ".exe"
| project TimeGenerated, RemoteUrl, RemoteIP, InitiatingProcessCommandLine
| order by TimeGenerated asc
| take 1

// FINDING: https://unresuscitating-donnette-smothery.ngrok-free.dev/revshell.exe
// SIGNIFICANCE: Attacker using ngrok tunnel to deliver payload
// TECHNIQUE: Dynamic DNS tunnel bypasses traditional blocklists
// IMPLICATION: Attacker has control over ngrok endpoint
// NEXT STEP: Identify which process executed the downloaded binary

// ===========================================
// FLAG 18: EXECUTION OF THE STAGED UNSIGNED BINARY
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// Shortly after the file was retrieved from the external tunnel, Defender
// recorded its execution on CH-OPS-WKS02.
//
// The binary did not originate from any trusted software distribution
// channel and was launched directly from the user's profile directory.
//
// This execution event marks the attacker's shift from staging to actively
// running their tooling.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Determine which process executed the downloaded binary.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Look for execution of revshell.exe
// The parent process is a common Windows shell component
// It was launched in a way resembling typical user interaction
// Execution happens after curl activity
// ===========================================

DeviceProcessEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where FileName == "revshell.exe"
| project 
    TimeGenerated,
    FileName,
    ProcessCommandLine,
    InitiatingProcessFileName,
    InitiatingProcessCommandLine,
    AccountName
| order by TimeGenerated asc
| take 1

// FINDING: explorer.exe
// SIGNIFICANCE: Launched via Windows Explorer (user-initiated or automated)
// IMPLICATION: Attacker has interactive desktop access OR script mimicking user
// TECHNIQUE: Using explorer.exe as parent makes execution appear benign
// NEXT STEP: Identify external C2 infrastructure

// ===========================================
// FLAG 19: IDENTIFY THE EXTERNAL IP CONTACTED BY THE EXECUTABLE
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// After execution on CH-OPS-WKS02, the downloaded binary attempted to
// initiate outbound communication to an external endpoint.
//
// Defender logged multiple failed TCP connection attempts to a remote IP
// on a high-nonstandard port.
//
// This traffic does not match any approved services and was initiated
// directly by the suspicious executable.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify the external IP address the process attempted to contact.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Filter DeviceNetworkEvents by executable name (revshell.exe)
// Look for ConnectionFailed or ConnectionAttempted events
// The destination port was 11746 (non-standard)
// ===========================================

DeviceNetworkEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where InitiatingProcessFileName == "revshell.exe"
| where ActionType in ("ConnectionAttempted", "ConnectionFailed", "ConnectionSuccess")
| where RemotePort == 11746
| summarize 
    FirstAttempt = min(TimeGenerated),
    AttemptCount = count()
    by RemoteIP, RemotePort
| project RemoteIP, RemotePort, FirstAttempt, AttemptCount

// FINDING: 13.228.171.119
// SIGNIFICANCE: External C2 server IP (AWS infrastructure)
// PORT: 11746 (high non-standard port, typical for C2)
// CONNECTION: Multiple failed attempts suggest firewall blocking
// NEXT STEP: Identify persistence mechanism for the executable

// ===========================================
// FLAG 20: PERSISTENCE VIA STARTUP FOLDER PLACEMENT
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// After the downloaded binary executed and attempted outbound communication,
// Defender recorded another file event involving the same executable.
//
// The file was copied into a Windows Startup directory - a location
// frequently abused by attackers for simple persistence.
//
// Anything placed in this directory launches automatically at user logon.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify the exact folder path where the attacker attempted to establish
// persistence for the executable.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Search DeviceFileEvents for .exe files written outside user profile
// Look for directories containing "Start"
// Path begins with C:\ProgramData\...
// ===========================================

DeviceFileEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where FileName == "revshell.exe"
| where FolderPath contains "StartUp" or FolderPath contains "Start Menu"
| where ActionType in ("FileCreated", "FileCopied")
| project TimeGenerated, FolderPath, ActionType, InitiatingProcessFileName

// FINDING: C:\ProgramData\Microsoft\Windows\Start Menu\Programs\StartUp\revshell.exe
// SIGNIFICANCE: Automatic execution at ANY user logon
// PERSISTENCE: Survives reboots, runs with user privileges
// TECHNIQUE: Classic startup folder persistence (T1547.001)
// NEXT STEP: Trace back to identify initial access method

// ===========================================
// PHASE TRANSITION: TRACING INITIAL ACCESS
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// With the attacker able to launch the malicious executable through
// explorer.exe, it becomes clear that they had interactive access to the
// desktop environment.
//
// This level of access typically indicates a remote session such as RDP
// or similar remote-interaction tooling.
//
// To understand how the compromise began, we now work BACKWARD from the
// established persistence and C2 activity toward the earlier session
// metadata recorded on the device.
//
// Defender logs show multiple events tied to a Remote Session Device Name
// and Remote Session IP. These values appear consistently across several
// earlier suspicious actions, suggesting a common origin point for the
// attacker's presence.
//
// This section focuses on identifying that source and retracing how the
// adversary first gained access to CH-OPS-WKS02.
// ===========================================

// ===========================================
// FLAG 21: IDENTIFY THE REMOTE SESSION SOURCE DEVICE
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// Several suspicious events - including file placement, network attempts,
// and execution - share the same remote session metadata.
//
// This indicates the attacker interacted with CH-OPS-WKS02 through a
// remote session rather than local physical access.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify the device name consistently listed as the remote session origin.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Look at previous KQL results where InitiatingProcessRemoteSessionDeviceName
// is populated. The same value appears repeatedly across several event types.
// The remote session field does not represent a hostname - treat it as
// the session label used by the connection.
// ===========================================

DeviceProcessEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where isnotempty(InitiatingProcessRemoteSessionDeviceName)
| summarize 
    EventCount = count(),
    FirstSeen = min(TimeGenerated),
    LastSeen = max(TimeGenerated)
    by InitiatingProcessRemoteSessionDeviceName
| order by EventCount desc

// FINDING: 对手 (Chinese characters meaning "adversary")
// SIGNIFICANCE: Attacker deliberately set remote device name to "adversary"
// ATTRIBUTION: Clear indication of deliberate hostile activity
// IMPLICATION: This is NOT a misconfigured admin - this is intentional compromise
// NEXT STEP: Identify source IP of remote session

// ===========================================
// FLAG 22: IDENTIFY THE REMOTE SESSION IP ADDRESS
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// The same remote session metadata attached to multiple suspicious events
// on CH-OPS-WKS02 includes a consistent originating IP address.
//
// This value represents the network source used by the adversary to
// interact with the system.
//
// Identifying this IP allows you to track the adversary's entry point and
// correlate it with authentication logs, lateral movement, or external
// access patterns.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify the IP address that appears as the source of the remote session
// tied to the attacker's activity.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Look for InitiatingProcessRemoteSessionIP fields
// The value is identical across all suspicious events
// The IP is not part of your internal 10.x address space
// ===========================================

DeviceProcessEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where InitiatingProcessRemoteSessionDeviceName == "对手"
| where isnotempty(InitiatingProcessRemoteSessionIP)
| summarize count() by InitiatingProcessRemoteSessionIP
| order by count_ desc

// FINDING: 100.64.100.6
// SIGNIFICANCE: CGNAT/relay range IP (100.64.0.0/10)
// IMPLICATION: Connection through carrier-grade NAT or cloud relay
// TECHNIQUE: Using shared IP space to obscure true origin
// NEXT STEP: Identify internal pivot host

// ===========================================
// FLAG 23: IDENTIFY THE INTERNAL PIVOT HOST USED BY THE ATTACKER
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// The remote session metadata shows multiple IP addresses associated with
// the attacker's activity.
//
// One of these addresses appears to be part of the internal Azure virtual
// network, suggesting the adversary either compromised another VM first
// or used an internal hop to reach CH-OPS-WKS02.
//
// Identifying this internal pivot point is essential to retracing the
// attacker's route through the environment.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify which internal IP address (non-100.64.x.x) appears as part of
// the attacker's remote session metadata.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Internal Azure subnets typically start with 10.x.x.x
// Exclude IPs in 100.64.0.0/10 CGNAT/relay range
// Use | distinct InitiatingProcessRemoteSessionIP to see all unique IPs
// ===========================================

DeviceProcessEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where isnotempty(InitiatingProcessRemoteSessionIP)
| distinct InitiatingProcessRemoteSessionIP
| where InitiatingProcessRemoteSessionIP startswith "10." // Internal Azure subnet
| where InitiatingProcessRemoteSessionIP !startswith "100.64" // Exclude CGNAT

// FINDING: 10.168.0.7
// SIGNIFICANCE: Internal Azure VM used as pivot point
// LATERAL MOVEMENT: Attacker compromised another system first
// ATTACK PATH: External → 10.168.0.7 → CH-OPS-WKS02
// NEXT STEP: Identify first suspicious logon timestamp

// ===========================================
// FLAG 24: IDENTIFY THE FIRST SUSPICIOUS LOGON EVENT
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// To determine when the adversary FIRST accessed the system, we need to
// look at the earliest logon event tied to their activity.
//
// This marks the true beginning of their presence on CH-OPS-WKS02.
//
// Multiple remote session IPs appear later in the attack timeline, but
// only one timestamp reflects the very first successful logon.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify the earliest timestamp showing a suspicious logon to CH-OPS-WKS02.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Use DeviceLogonEvents
// Sort by timestamp ascending
// Logon types of interest: RemoteInteractive (10), Network (3), RDP-style
// Look for accounts involved in later suspicious activity
// ===========================================

DeviceLogonEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where LogonType in ("RemoteInteractive", "Network", "Unlock") // Logon types 10, 3, etc.
| where AccountName in ("chadmin", "ops.maintenance") // Suspicious accounts
| project TimeGenerated, AccountName, LogonType, RemoteIP, RemoteDeviceName
| order by TimeGenerated asc
| take 1

// FINDING: 2025-11-23T03:08:31.1849379Z
// SIGNIFICANCE: TRUE START of the intrusion
// TIMELINE: ~38 minutes BEFORE first beacon (03:46:08Z)
// IMPLICATION: Attacker had 38 minutes of reconnaissance before deploying tools
// NEXT STEP: Identify source IP of first logon

// ===========================================
// FLAG 25: IP ADDRESS USED DURING THE FIRST SUSPICIOUS LOGON
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// After determining when the attacker first logged in, the next step is
// identifying where they logged in FROM.
//
// The earliest logon event provides a direct indicator of the attacker's
// initial network origin.
//
// This IP represents the start of the intrusion path and will help narrow
// down whether the access came from another internal VM, a pivot device,
// or an external relay.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify the IP address associated with the earliest suspicious logon.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Use the timestamp from previous flag as anchor point
// Look for RemoteIP field in that exact logon event
// Compare against other later logons to confirm it is the earliest
// ===========================================

DeviceLogonEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where TimeGenerated == datetime(2025-11-23T03:08:31.1849379Z)
| project RemoteIP

// FINDING: 104.164.168.17
// SIGNIFICANCE: External public IP - attacker's true origin point
// GEOGRAPHY: Will be enriched in later flag
// IMPLICATION: Direct external access, not lateral movement
// NEXT STEP: Identify compromised account used for initial access

// ===========================================
// FLAG 26: ACCOUNT USED DURING THE FIRST SUSPICIOUS LOGON
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// Knowing the timestamp and IP of the initial suspicious logon allows us
// to pinpoint exactly which credentials the adversary leveraged to gain access.
//
// Identifying the compromised account is critical for understanding how
// the attacker authenticated and whether they used stolen credentials,
// a shared admin account, or a local user.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Determine which account name appears in the earliest suspicious logon event.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Use the earliest timestamp from previous flag to anchor search
// Look at AccountName field for that exact logon event
// Ensure you are looking only at that first event, not the ones that follow
// ===========================================

DeviceLogonEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where TimeGenerated == datetime(2025-11-23T03:08:31.1849379Z)
| project AccountName

// FINDING: chadmin
// SIGNIFICANCE: "chadmin" account - likely "Chinese admin" or similar
// IMPLICATION: Attacker gained credentials for administrative account
// NEXT ACCESS: Later switches to ops.maintenance for privileged operations
// NEXT STEP: Geolocate attacker's origin

// ===========================================
// FLAG 27: DETERMINE THE ATTACKER'S GEOGRAPHIC REGION
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// The suspicious remote device authenticated into CH-OPS-WKS02 using
// several public IPs within a range.
//
// To understand where the attacker was operating from, analysts often
// enrich these IPs with geolocation data.
//
// Microsoft's geo_info_from_ip_address() function allows you to derive
// country, region, and city information directly from KQL - no external
// OSINT tools required.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Determine the attacker's geographic origin using geolocation enrichment.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Use suspicious IPs from previous flags
// Use geo_info_from_ip_address(RemoteIP) to reveal country data
// All suspicious IPs belong to same network range and map to same region
// ===========================================

let SuspiciousIPs = dynamic(["104.164.168.17", "100.64.100.6", "10.168.0.7"]);
DeviceLogonEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where RemoteIP in (SuspiciousIPs) or RemoteIP == "104.164.168.17"
| extend GeoInfo = geo_info_from_ip_address(RemoteIP)
| extend Country = tostring(GeoInfo.country)
| summarize count() by Country
| where isnotempty(Country)

// FINDING: Vietnam
// SIGNIFICANCE: Attacker operating from Vietnamese IP space
// ATTRIBUTION: Combined with device name "对手" (Chinese) - possible misdirection
// INTELLIGENCE: Vietnam is known source of APT activity
// NEXT STEP: Identify attacker's first action after logon

// ===========================================
// FLAG 28: FIRST PROCESS LAUNCHED AFTER THE ATTACKER LOGGED IN
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// After establishing the attacker's first login timestamp and origin IP,
// the next step is determining what they did IMMEDIATELY after gaining access.
//
// Defender records each new process execution along with its associated
// logon session, allowing analysts to trace the attacker's first action
// on the system.
//
// This reveals whether the adversary began with exploration, privilege
// escalation, or immediate deployment of tools.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify the first process launched by the attacker immediately after
// logging in.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Use timestamp from earliest suspicious logon
// Search DeviceProcessEvents for processes whose AccountName matches session
// Sort by timestamp ascending and pick first process after login
// Remember to reference AccountName AND InitiatingProcessAccountName
// Earliest processes are often: cmd.exe, explorer.exe, notepad.exe
// ===========================================

let FirstLogonTime = datetime(2025-11-23T03:08:31.1849379Z);
DeviceProcessEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where TimeGenerated >= FirstLogonTime
| where AccountName == "chadmin" or InitiatingProcessAccountName == "chadmin"
| project TimeGenerated, FileName, ProcessCommandLine, AccountName, InitiatingProcessAccountName
| order by TimeGenerated asc
| take 1

// FINDING: explorer.exe
// SIGNIFICANCE: Windows Explorer launched - indicates GUI/desktop access
// IMPLICATION: Attacker has full interactive session, not just command-line
// TECHNIQUE: RDP or similar remote desktop technology
// NEXT STEP: Identify first file accessed

// ===========================================
// FLAG 29: IDENTIFY THE FIRST FILE THE ATTACKER ACCESSED
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// Once the attacker authenticated into the system, their very first action
// can reveal their priorities and objectives.
//
// Early file access is often a strong indicator of what the attacker was
// searching for - credentials, configuration data, operational details,
// or system weaknesses.
//
// By examining the earliest file opened within the session, you can
// identify exactly what they were after.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify what file the attacker opened first after the previous flag.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Use earliest suspicious logon timestamp as anchor point
// Look at DeviceProcessEvents for processes with file arguments in command line
// Attacker opened file using GUI application (not command-line)
// Reference previous flag's ProcessID to find this flag's InitiatingProcessID
// ===========================================

let FirstProcessPID = 1234; // Replace with actual PID from Flag 28 results
DeviceProcessEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where InitiatingProcessId == FirstProcessPID
| where ProcessCommandLine contains ".txt" or ProcessCommandLine contains "notepad"
| extend OpenedFile = extract(@'([A-Za-z0-9_\-\s]+\.txt)', 1, ProcessCommandLine)
| where isnotempty(OpenedFile)
| project TimeGenerated, OpenedFile, ProcessCommandLine
| order by TimeGenerated asc
| take 1

// FINDING: CH-OPS-WKS02 user-pass.txt
// SIGNIFICANCE: Credential file containing usernames and passwords
// IMPLICATION: Attacker IMMEDIATELY went for stored credentials
// TECHNIQUE: Reconnaissance → credential harvesting
// NEXT STEP: Determine what attacker did with discovered credentials

// ===========================================
// FLAG 30: DETERMINE THE ATTACKER'S NEXT ACTION AFTER READING THE FILE
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// After viewing the file, the attacker moved on to their next step in
// the intrusion chain.
//
// Early post-logon behavior often reveals operational intent - whether
// they used stolen credentials, attempted lateral movement, escalated
// privileges, or launched additional tooling.
//
// By examining process execution following the previous activity, analysts
// can determine how the attacker leveraged the information found in the file.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify what the attacker did next after reading the file.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Use timestamp of previous activity as anchor
// Search for next process execution immediately after file was opened
// Next action may be:
// - launching command shell (cmd.exe)
// - attempting another logon
// - executing recon commands (ipconfig, whoami, net user)
// - initiating lateral movement
// ===========================================

let FileOpenTime = datetime(2025-11-23T03:08:35Z); // Approximate based on first process
DeviceProcessEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where TimeGenerated > FileOpenTime
| where AccountName == "chadmin"
| where FileName in ("ipconfig.exe", "whoami.exe", "net.exe", "nltest.exe", "cmd.exe", "powershell.exe")
| project TimeGenerated, FileName, ProcessCommandLine
| order by TimeGenerated asc
| take 1

// FINDING: ipconfig.exe
// SIGNIFICANCE: Network reconnaissance - mapping local network configuration
// TECHNIQUE: Standard enumeration command
// PATTERN: Credentials → Recon → Privilege escalation (typical attack flow)
// NEXT STEP: Identify next account accessed after recon

// ===========================================
// FLAG 31: IDENTIFY THE NEXT ACCOUNT ACCESSED AFTER RECON
// ===========================================
// INVESTIGATION CONTEXT:
// Following the attacker's first round of local reconnaissance, the
// intrusion shifted from information-gathering to account-level interaction.
//
// This transition often marks the moment an adversary tests stolen
// credentials, pivots to a higher-value user profile, or begins lateral
// preparation.
//
// By analyzing logon activity immediately after the enumeration window,
// defenders can pinpoint which account the attacker chose to access next -
// a critical step in reconstructing intent and privilege escalation paths.
//
// YOUR TASK:
// Identify which user account the attacker accessed immediately after
// their initial enumeration activity.
//
// ANALYST THINKING:
// Anchor time window to the moment enumeration completed
// Look in DeviceLogonEvents for next successful logon event after that timestamp
// Filter by suspicious remote session device name or IP (same one identified earlier)
// You're looking for the next ACCOUNT used, not the next process
// ===========================================

let ReconCompleteTime = datetime(2025-11-23T03:10:00Z); // After ipconfig
DeviceLogonEvents
| where DeviceName == "ch-ops-wks02"
| where TimeGenerated > ReconCompleteTime
| where AccountName != "chadmin" // Different account
| project TimeGenerated, AccountName, LogonType, RemoteIP
| order by TimeGenerated asc
| take 1

// FINDING: ops.maintenance
// SIGNIFICANCE: Switched to operational maintenance account with elevated privileges
// ATTACK PROGRESSION: chadmin (initial access) → ops.maintenance (privileged operations)
// TECHNIQUE: Credential abuse using stolen passwords from user-pass.txt file
// TIMELINE COMPLETE: Initial access → Recon → Credential use → Privilege ops → Tool deployment

// ===========================================
// HUNT CLOSURE & INVESTIGATION SUMMARY
// ===========================================
// FINAL ANALYSIS:
// By working backward through the attacker's activity - from persistence
// artifacts to reconnaissance actions, then retracing initial access -
// the full intrusion chain becomes clear.
//
// ATTACK TIMELINE RECONSTRUCTION:
// 1. Initial Access (03:08:31Z) - External IP 104.164.168.17 → chadmin account
// 2. Reconnaissance (03:08-03:10) - explorer.exe → user-pass.txt → ipconfig.exe
// 3. Credential Abuse (03:10+) - Switch to ops.maintenance account
// 4. Tool Deployment (03:46:08Z) - MaintenanceRunner_Distributed.ps1 beaconing
// 5. Privilege Escalation (03:47:21Z) - Token modification, ConfigAdjust
// 6. Persistence (03:47-04:00) - Scheduled tasks, Run keys, registry manipulation
// 7. Tool Transfer (03:50+) - curl.exe → revshell.exe from ngrok tunnel
// 8. C2 Communication (ongoing) - Attempted connections to 13.228.171.119:11746
// 9. Final Persistence (04:00+) - revshell.exe → Startup folder
//
// INDICATORS OF COMPROMISE (IOCs):
// - External IPs: 104.164.168.17 (initial), 13.228.171.119 (C2)
// - Internal Pivot: 10.168.0.7
// - Compromised Accounts: chadmin, ops.maintenance
// - Malicious Files:
//   - MaintenanceRunner_Distributed.ps1
//   - revshell.exe (SHA256: [from Flag 16])
//   - inventory_6ECFD4DF.csv (SHA256: 7f6393568e414fc564dad6f49a06a161618b50873404503f82c4447d239f12d8)
//   - inventory_tmp_6ECFD4DF.csv (different hash)
// - C2 Infrastructure:
//   - 127.0.0.1:8080 (local proxy/tunnel)
//   - https://unresuscitating-donnette-smothery.ngrok-free.dev/
//   - 13.228.171.119:11746 (AWS-hosted C2)
// - Persistence Mechanisms:
//   - Scheduled Task: CorpHealth_A65E64
//   - Run Key: MaintenanceRunner
//   - Startup Folder: revshell.exe
//
// MITRE ATT&CK MAPPING:
// - T1078.003 - Valid Accounts: Local Accounts (chadmin, ops.maintenance)
// - T1133 - External Remote Services (RDP from Vietnam)
// - T1087 - Account Discovery (ipconfig, recon commands)
// - T1552.001 - Credentials in Files (user-pass.txt)
// - T1548.002 - Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism: Bypass UAC (token modification)
// - T1053.005 - Scheduled Task/Job: Scheduled Task
// - T1547.001 - Boot or Logon Autostart: Registry Run Keys
// - T1059.001 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: PowerShell
// - T1027 - Obfuscated Files or Information (Base64 encoded commands)
// - T1562.001 - Impair Defenses: Disable or Modify Tools (AV exclusion attempt)
// - T1105 - Ingress Tool Transfer (curl → revshell.exe)
// - T1071.001 - Application Layer Protocol: Web Protocols (ngrok tunnel)
// - T1041 - Exfiltration Over C2 Channel (staged inventory files)
//
// CASE CLASSIFICATION CHANGE:
// Initial: "Operations Activity Review"
// Final: Confirmed Intrusion - External Actor, Credential Theft, C2 Established
//
// RECOMMENDED ACTIONS:
// 1. Force password reset for ALL accounts on CH-OPS-WKS02
// 2. Revoke ops.maintenance elevated privileges pending review
// 3. Block IOC IPs at firewall: 104.164.168.17, 13.228.171.119
// 4. Hunt for 10.168.0.7 compromise (internal pivot point)
